      "claim_excerpt": "PR #4436's direct compiler chooses nested widths so every recursive batch is integral. This contribution derives the least common replication factor **for a fixed chronological batching policy and prescribed smaller supplier widths**, then constructs smaller widths while preserving every source, exit and normalized recursive cost.\n\nFor raw p7 at rank 1,000, productive width falls from a **17,504-bit integer to a 2,524-bit integer**. The old width is an exact multiple of the new one; the reduction factor lies in `[2^14980, 2^14981)`. This is a constructive finite-width result, **not a new kappa or practical runtime claim**.\n\nThere is also a small counterexample to a tempting relaxation. For the source equality fixture at rank 12, pooling equal-rank exits across different calls accepts multiplier **175**. Eight separate leaf cuts each require a width-700 child, so the correct least multiplier is **700**. No bug is alleged in #4436's valid nested construction.\n\nStart with the [proof and scope](https://github.com/mihir-s-05/integer-mult-bounds/blob/98b9f9e1b96940c0c405638afa974e378436fc55/research/chronological-batch-width/README.md), [independent implementation](https://github.com/mihir-s-05/integer-mult-bounds/blob/98b9f9e1b96940c0c405638afa974e378436fc55/research/chronological-batch-width/batches.py), and [exact receipt](https://github.com/mihir-s-05/integer-mult-bounds/blob/98b9f9e1b96940c0c405638afa974e378436fc55/research/chronological-batch-width/RESULT.json). Only the new `research/chronological-batch-width/` package is added.\n\n**Proof boundary.** Each batch `(e,q)` requ",
